{"id":"security-security-checklist","corpus":"k8s","title":"Security Checklist","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/security-checklist/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Security Checklist"},{"kind":"p","text":"Baseline checklist for ensuring security in Kubernetes clusters."},{"kind":"p","text":"This checklist aims at providing a basic list of guidance with links to more comprehensive documentation on each topic. It does not claim to be exhaustive and is meant to evolve."},{"kind":"p","text":"On how to read and use this document:"},{"kind":"li","text":"The order of topics does not reflect an order of priority."},{"kind":"li","text":"Some checklist items are detailed in the paragraph below the list of each section."},{"kind":"p","text":"Checklists are not sufficient for attaining a good security posture on their own. A good security posture requires constant attention and improvement, but a checklist can be the first step on the never-ending journey towards security preparedness. Some of the recommendations in this checklist may be too restrictive or too lax for your specific security needs. Since Kubernetes security is not \"one size fits all\", each category of checklist items should be evaluated on its merits."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Authentication & Authorization"},{"kind":"li","text":"system:masters group is not used for user or component authentication after bootstrapping."},{"kind":"li","text":"The kube-controller-manager is running with --use-service-account-credentials enabled."},{"kind":"li","text":"The root certificate is protected (either an offline CA, or a managed online CA with effective access controls)."},{"kind":"li","text":"Intermediate and leaf certificates have an expiry date no more than 3 years in the future."},{"kind":"li","text":"A process exists for periodic access review, and reviews occur no more than 24 months apart."},{"kind":"li","text":"The Role Based Access Control Good Practices are followed for guidance related to authentication and authorization."},{"kind":"p","text":"After bootstrapping, neither users nor components should authenticate to the Kubernetes API as system:masters. Similarly, running all of kube-controller-manager as system:masters should be avoided. In fact, system:masters should only be used as a break-glass mechanism, as opposed to an admin user."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Network security"},{"kind":"li","text":"CNI plugins in use support network policies."},{"kind":"li","text":"Ingress and egress network policies are applied to all workloads in the cluster."},{"kind":"li","text":"Default network policies within each namespace, selecting all pods, denying everything, are in place."},{"kind":"li","text":"If appropriate, a service mesh is used to encrypt all communications inside of the cluster."},{"kind":"li","text":"The Kubernetes API, kubelet API and etcd are not exposed publicly on Internet."},{"kind":"li","text":"Access from the workloads to the cloud metadata API is filtered."},{"kind":"li","text":"Use of LoadBalancer and ExternalIPs is restricted."},{"kind":"p","text":"A number of Container Network Interface (CNI) plugins plugins provide the functionality to restrict network resources that pods may communicate with. This is most commonly done through Network Policies which provide a namespaced resource to define rules. Default network policies that block all egress and ingress, in each namespace, selecting all pods, can be useful to adopt an allow list approach to ensure that no workloads are missed."},{"kind":"p","text":"Not all CNI plugins provide encryption in transit. If the chosen plugin lacks this feature, an alternative solution could be to use a service mesh to provide that functionality."},{"kind":"p","text":"The etcd datastore of the control plane should have controls to limit access and not be publicly exposed on the Internet. Furthermore, mutual TLS (mTLS) should be used to communicate securely with it. The certificate authority for this should be unique to etcd."},{"kind":"p","text":"External Internet access to the Kubernetes API server should be restricted to not expose the API publicly. Be careful, as many managed Kubernetes distributions are publicly exposing the API server by default. You can then use a bastion host to access the server."},{"kind":"p","text":"The kubelet API access should be restricted and not exposed publicly, the default authentication and authorization settings, when no configuration file specified with the --config flag, are overly permissive."},{"kind":"p","text":"If a cloud provider is used for hosting Kubernetes, the access from pods to the cloud metadata API 169.254.169.254 should also be restricted or blocked if not needed because it may leak information."},{"kind":"p","text":"For restricted LoadBalancer and ExternalIPs use, see CVE-2020-8554: Man in the middle using LoadBalancer or ExternalIPs and the DenyServiceExternalIPs admission controller for further information."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Pod security"},{"kind":"li","text":"RBAC rights to create, update, patch, delete workloads is only granted if necessary."},{"kind":"li","text":"Appropriate Pod Security Standards policy is applied for all namespaces and enforced."},{"kind":"li","text":"Memory limit is set for the workloads with a limit equal or inferior to the request."},{"kind":"li","text":"CPU limit might be set on sensitive workloads."},{"kind":"li","text":"For nodes that support it, Seccomp is enabled with appropriate syscalls profile for programs."}],"lemmas":{"securities":["security","securities"],"baselines":["baseline","baselines"],"ensuring":["ensuring","ensure"],"ensurings":["ensurings","ensuring"],"ensures":["ensures","ensure"],"ensured":["ensured","ensure"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"clusters":["cluster","clusters"],"clustering":["clustering","cluster"],"c